BH i.Booster bridge - Phase 1

SETUP
Copy src\wifi_config.example.h to src\wifi_config.h, then edit WIFI_SSID
and WIFI_PASS in src\wifi_config.h before building. This local file is
ignored by Git; do not put real credentials in the example file.
IP: 192.168.218.61/24, gateway/DNS: 192.168.218.1.
Reserve that address outside the DHCP pool to avoid conflicts.
Build: pio run
Upload (only with the intended board connected): pio run -t upload
The custom partitions.csv provides two 1.875 MiB OTA application slots;
upload also writes the partition table so the firmware fits the device.
USB console: pio device monitor -b 115200
Open http://192.168.218.61 or http://cinta.local after Wi-Fi connects.
No boot-time BLE connection or automatic reconnection is attempted.
NimBLE controller initialization is deferred until Wi-Fi has associated,
so simultaneous radio startup cannot prevent the web server from booting.

WEB/API
POST /api/ble/scan: 202; scan runs for 5 seconds in a separate BLE task.
GET /api/status: scanGeneration, devices (name/address/type/rssi/target),
state, errors, GATT UUIDs, requested targets and telemetry.
POST /api/ble/connect: {"address":"AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF","type":0}
Use the address type from the scan (0 public, 1 random).
An empty object selects PAFERS_C7BE99 from the last scan.
POST /api/ble/disconnect: {}
POST /api/control/start or /api/control/stop: {}
POST /api/control/speed: {"value":41} means 4.1 km/h.
POST /api/control/incline: {"value":3} means level 3.
The web UI provides direct incline buttons for levels 1 through 15.
Speed accepts relative deltas {"delta":1}, {"delta":-1}, {"delta":5},
{"delta":-5} (0.1 or 0.5 km/h); inclination accepts +/-1 level.
Relative adjustments are applied sequentially in the worker, not against
a stale browser snapshot. Limits: 0-16 km/h, incline targets 1-15.
The reference initialization still sends incline 0; a speed change preserves
that last initialized value until an incline target is explicitly set.
STOP discards queued commands and takes the next worker slot; it cannot
interrupt a GATT transaction already in progress.
POST /api/ble/raw: {"hex":"55 0A 01 02"}, literal 1-20 bytes.
GET /api/logs?since=0: bounded 64-entry HEX/event ring; lost marks overrun.
USB output is nonblocking; usbLogsDropped in status counts lines omitted when
the host is not reading. The bounded web log remains available.
202 means queued, NOT confirmed by the treadmill. Check status and logs.
No authentication/TLS: use only an isolated trusted LAN. Do not expose ports.

PROTOCOL LIMITATIONS / HARDWARE VALIDATION
Reference: https://github.com/cagnulein/qdomyos-zwift/tree/master/src/devices/paferstreadmill
This is an independent implementation of the wire format, not a copy of
the upstream implementation. These Pafers treadmill frames have no checksum.
The receive decoder follows the reference's 13-byte telemetry layout;
unknown packets remain visible in HEX. Fragmented/concatenated frames are
reassembled by the protocol module.
Fractional speed writes use a hundredths byte inferred from RX and are
EXPERIMENTAL. Validate 4.1 km/h against console and notifications first.
The handshake follows the base treadmill variant (not iBoxster Plus).
It sends STOP, a reference user profile, speed 1.0 and incline 0, but no
START command. It is not proof of device compatibility or a safety interlock.
Control is enabled after subscription and transmission of initialization,
not after a proprietary acknowledgement (not documented by the reference).
Some BH consoles report 1 km/h while stopped: that reading is marked
ambiguous and excluded from movement time/distance estimates.
Distance and time are integrated locally from fresh telemetry (<3 seconds),
reset per BLE connection, and are NOT decoded console counters.
Motion state is inferred or a console-button event, not an acknowledged
result of a web command. Unknown/stale data is explicitly displayed.
Verify the physical machine's speed/incline limits before use.
Test with an empty belt, clear surroundings and the safety key accessible.
Web STOP and BLE disconnect are NOT emergency-stop mechanisms.
Firmware compilation cannot verify actual BH i.Booster compatibility.

TESTS
test_protocol.cpp is an independent host C++ test executable (no Arduino).
With a host compiler: g++ -std=c++11 test\test_protocol.cpp -o .pio\test_protocol.exe
Then run .pio\test_protocol.exe (0 = all checks passed).
It covers wire commands, experimental decimals, bounds, literal HEX,
handshake without START, telemetry validation and stream reassembly.
PAFERS_FREESTANDING_TEST provides a CRT-free Windows entry for the existing
Android NDK Clang toolchain when a desktop C++ runtime is not installed.
Do not upload these host tests to a connected treadmill.
